# How git is to treat what is in here.
#
# The content files are read by hand-written line parsers and are meant to be
# edited by people, so they are text and they are checked out with LF endings on
# every platform. That is not a style preference: a checkout that converts them
# to CRLF used to make every one of them unreadable, on Windows only, because a
# blank line in a CRLF file is a line holding a carriage return rather than a
# blank one. The parsers were fixed to cope - see the note in PROJECT_STATUS.md -
# and this stops the situation arising in the first place.
#
# Everything the game *writes* is binary and must never be touched.

# Sensible defaults: let git decide, and normalise to LF in the repository.
*               text=auto

# Content, and the code that reads it. LF on disk everywhere, so a file that
# round-trips through a Windows editor is the only way to get a CRLF one - and
# the game reads that too.
*.c             text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.cmake         text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt  text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf

# Anything the game or the baker writes. A byte changed in any of these is a
# corrupt file, not a line ending.
*.ggmap         binary
*.ggsave        binary
*.ggprof        binary
*.png           binary
*.bmp           binary
*.wav           binary
*.ttf           binary

# A replay is text on purpose - it is meant to be read, diffed and trimmed by
# hand before being pasted into a bug report - but it is written by the game, so
# it is left exactly as it was written.
*.ggreplay      -text
